# Supplementary Data: Kinematic-Solar Resonance Engine (1980–2026 Hindcast Log)

This document contains the structural hindcast calculations for the Kinematic-Solar Resonance Theory of ENSO. It models the year-over-year momentum shifts ($E_M$) from 1980 to 2026 using the fully bounded multi-variable equation.

### The Governing Equation
$$ E_M = \left( K + \Omega_{mem} \right) \times \Gamma(t) $$
$$ ENSO_t = \max(-10, \min(10, ENSO_{t-1} + E_M)) $$

### Variable Key & Mathematical Bounds
*   **$ENSO_{t-1}$ (Previous State):** The observed peak climate state of the prior year. Scale is bounded from `-10` (Super La Niña) to `+10` (Super El Niño).
*   **$K$ (Kinematic Anomaly):** Derived from the JPL heliocentric vector. `+1` (Earth receding from Jupiter during Spring/Summer; continental warming bias). `-1` (Earth approaching Jupiter; continental cooling bias).
*   **$\Omega_{mem}$ (Ocean Memory):** Detrended Warm Water Volume (WWV) potential. Bounded `[-5, +5]`. Cold/suppressed oceans yield positive (loaded spring) values; warm/exhausted oceans yield negative (dead battery) values.
*   **$\Gamma$ (Solar Amplifier):** Trailing derivative of sunspot acceleration. Bounded `[1, 3]`. Flat solar activity equals `1`. Rapid acceleration/deceleration equals `3`.
*   **$E_M$ (Calculated Delta):** The year-over-year momentum shift.
*   **Predicted vs. Actual:** The model's theoretical output compared to the historical NOAA Oceanic Niño Index (ONI) scaled equivalent.

*(Note: $\Omega_{mem}$ and $\Gamma$ values in this table are calibrated structural estimates to demonstrate the mathematical framework prior to the programmatic injection of raw CSV telemetry).*

